--- machine\system\network\rules
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules

Peios Network Policy: the firewall, as the kernel reads it. One subkey per
layer — `RawPacket`, `Packet`, `Flow`, and netd's `Interface` — each a
forest of rule keys.

The kernel reads this subtree whole when registryd registers, re-walks it
on change, and publishes the result as a numbered **generation**. A
generation is all-or-nothing: if any rule in any layer is malformed, names
a fact its layer cannot have, or breaks one of the laws, the whole walk is
refused and the previous generation stays in force. There is no partial
policy and no window without one. Until the first generation lands, the
compiled-in backstop is `DROP` in every layer.

A rule is a key. Its values are its conditions (`<Fact>.<Operator>`) plus
`Actions`, `Priority` and `Enabled`; its subkeys are exceptions, narrower
regions under the same laws. Collation is highest priority first, ties to
the strictest verdict, and an abstaining rule hands its parentage up.

The full vocabulary — every fact, operator, action, limit and the value
forms each accepts — is the network policy reference (learn: networking,
"Network policy reference"). This file documents the registry surface: the
keys, the fixed-name values, and what editing them costs.

Two things live next to the rules and are **not** policy: `Interfaces\` and
`Networks\`, which netd writes and the kernel reads as the network context
behind the `Network.*` facts. They are netd's keys and are documented
there.

Default security: inherits the Machine hive root (SYSTEM and
Administrators full, Authenticated Users read). That descriptor is the
whole gate on changing this machine's firewall — there is no second
permission model, and `reg set` against these keys is the same act as any
other authoring surface.

--- machine\system\network\rules currentreportinglevel
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules CurrentReportingLevel
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1 (absent — everything fires)
valid: 1–6
applies: live — at the next generation

How loud `REPORT(n)` is. A rule's `REPORT(n)` effect fires when
`n >= CurrentReportingLevel`, so raising this value silences the quieter
reports and leaves the louder ones.

`1` is everything and is what an absent value means; `6` silences every
report. Quietness ships as a visible value rather than a default, so a
machine that is not reporting says so in the registry.

Out of range refuses the generation — like any other malformed policy, and
for the same reason: a firewall that silently ignored what you wrote would
be worse than one that refused it.

This value lives on the `Rules` key itself, not on a layer: reporting
volume is machine-wide.

--- machine\system\network\rules\rawpacket
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\RawPacket

The wire-side layer: judged at the device seats, for all traffic, per
packet — first inbound, last outbound.

This is the layer that sees what actually arrives. Fragments may still be
fragments (`Fragment` may be 1), conntrack has not run, so there is no
`FlowState`, and none of the flow layer's facts (`Related`, `Start.*`,
`Local*`, `Remote.*`) exist here. Per-packet facts — `Length`, `TcpFlags`,
`Ttl`, `Dscp`, `EtherType`, `DstMac` — all do.

It may write tags and it may not read any: a `RawPacket` rule reading a tag
is a downward read and refuses the generation.

Absent, this layer has no rules and contributes nothing. The backstop
still applies.

--- machine\system\network\rules\packet
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\Packet

The per-packet layer: judged once per traversal, at the richest seat —
between the wire and the flow.

The ordinary place to write packet policy. Everything is reassembled
(`Fragment` is always 0) and conntrack has run, so `FlowState` is
available here and nowhere else below the flow layer. The per-packet facts
are all present; the flow layer's are not.

It may write tags, and read tags written by `RawPacket` or `Packet` rules
— never one a `Flow` rule wrote.

Absent, this layer has no rules and contributes nothing. The backstop
still applies.

--- machine\system\network\rules\flow
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\Flow

The flow layer: judged once per flow, per local endpoint, at the IP seats,
on the flow's first packet — last inbound, first outbound.

Its verdict is cached on the flow as that flow's **sentence**, so this is
where a decision about a conversation belongs rather than a decision
about a packet. It is the only layer with `Related`, `Start.*`, `Local`,
`Local.*` and `Remote.*` — the facts about who is at this end — and the
only one that may read any tag, whichever layer wrote it. It has no
per-packet facts at all: `Length`, `TcpFlags` and the rest are never here,
because a flow is not a packet.

Effects run per evaluation of the flow, not per packet.

Absent, this layer has no rules and contributes nothing. The backstop
still applies.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...>

One rule. The key name is attribution — it names the decision and is what
the viewer and the event stream quote back at you — and carries no meaning
to the engine.

Its values are its conditions, one per `<Fact>.<Operator>` name, plus the
three fixed names below. A rule matches when every condition holds; a
condition over a fact the packet lacks is false, which is the absent-fact
law and why `Present` is the one operator that looks through it. A value
name that is neither a fixed name nor a well-formed `<Fact>.<Operator>` is
an error that refuses the generation — there is no ignoring an unknown
value here.

Three things hold for every condition, and are not repeated on each fact's
page. `Present` takes `1` (the fact exists for this packet or flow) or `0`
(it does not); on a fact its layer never has it refuses the generation
rather than becoming an always-true condition. `Equal` against a
`REG_MULTI_SZ` list means any of, and an integer list's elements may be
`a-b` ranges. Applying an operator a family does not support —
`GreaterThan` on an address, `Has` on a port — refuses the generation.

Every fact has its own page: `regman <this key> DstPort.Equal` and the
`Values` index below both reach them.

Its subkeys are **exceptions**: a narrower region judged under the same
laws, inheriting `Priority` unless it names its own. Nesting runs to 12
deep, and a layer holds at most 4096 rules; exceeding either refuses the
generation.

The `<rule...>` in this page's path is that nesting — one rule name or a
whole chain of them. An exception is a rule, with the same values meaning
the same things, so this is its page too however deep it sits.

This page covers all four layers. netd builds and judges `Rules\Interface`
with `pnp-core`, the kernel's own engine, so a rule key means the same
thing there — but that layer's facts, verdicts and effects are its own,
and are on its page.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> actions
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Actions
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (absent or empty — NULL, the rule abstains)
valid: one action expression per element, from the layer's action vocabulary
applies: live — at the next generation

What the rule does when it matches: the action list, one expression per
element, evaluated in order.

An absent or empty `Actions` is `NULL` — the rule abstains. That is not a
mistake and not a no-op: an abstaining rule hands its parentage up the
tree, which is how an exception says "not this one, ask my parent again".

An expression the layer does not support — a packet verdict in the
interface layer, a `TAG` read where only writes are legal — refuses the
generation rather than being dropped.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> priority
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Priority
type: REG_DWORD or REG_QWORD
default: 0 at a root; a subkey inherits its parent's
valid: any integer the type holds
applies: live — at the next generation

Collation weight: the highest priority wins.

A subkey inherits this unless it sets its own, so a tree of exceptions
ranks together without repeating the number on every key. Ties go to the
strictest verdict — which in the packet layers is a total order, and in
the interface layer is `DOWN` > `IGNORE` > `JOIN`. A tie between two rules
naming different profiles for one interface has no strictest answer and
refuses the generation instead of picking.

A value that is not an integer refuses the generation.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> enabled
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Enabled
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1 (enabled)
valid: 0 or 1
applies: live — at the next generation

Whether the rule is in the policy at all.

`0` removes the rule **and its whole subtree** from the generation — every
exception under it goes with it. That makes this the safe way to take a
rule out of force while keeping it, and its authoring, where somebody can
read it.

Anything other than 0 or 1 refuses the generation.
--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> direction.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Direction.<op>
type: REG_SZ, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list meaning any of
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, Present; in, out
applies: live — at the next generation

Which way the packet is going.

At the `Flow` layer this is the originator's side and keeps that value
for the flow's whole life, so an inbound flow stays `in` for the replies
too.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> interface.equal
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Interface.Equal
type: REG_SZ, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list meaning any of
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: an interface name, e.g. eth0
applies: live — at the next generation

The interface the packet crossed, by kernel name.

Present at every layer. On an outbound `Flow` judgment it is the device
the route chose.

This is the one packet fact the `Interface` layer shares with the packet
layers; the rest of that layer's vocabulary is `Interface.*`.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> interface.present
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Interface.Present
type: REG_DWORD, or a decimal string
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: 1 the fact exists, 0 it does not
applies: live — at the next generation

The interface the packet crossed, by kernel name.

Present at every layer. On an outbound `Flow` judgment it is the device
the route chose.

This is the one packet fact the `Interface` layer shares with the packet
layers; the rest of that layer's vocabulary is `Interface.*`.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> ethertype.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> EtherType.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; a number, or a name
applies: live — at the next generation

The frame's EtherType.

Values: a number, or `ipv4`, `ipv6`, `arp`.

Not a `Flow` fact: a flow's family is its addresses', so a `Flow` rule
conditioning on this can never match.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> srcmac.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> SrcMac.<op>
type: REG_SZ, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list meaning any of
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, Present; aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff
applies: live — at the next generation

The source hardware address.

Present when the frame has an Ethernet header. At the IP seats outbound
that is the machine's own device address, not a peer's.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> dstmac.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> DstMac.<op>
type: REG_SZ, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list meaning any of
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, Present; aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff
applies: live — at the next generation

The destination hardware address.

Present when the frame has an Ethernet header. Not a `Flow` fact.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> vlan.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Vlan.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; a VLAN id
applies: live — at the next generation

The frame's VLAN id.

Present when the frame is VLAN-tagged (at the device seats) or the
interface is a VLAN device (at the IP seats).

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> srcaddr.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> SrcAddr.<op>
type: REG_SZ, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list meaning any of
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, Present; an address, a CIDR prefix, or an IPv4 range
applies: live — at the next generation

The source IP address.

Values: an exact address (`10.0.0.5`, `fd00::1`), a CIDR prefix
(`10.0.0.0/8`, `fd00::/64`), or an IPv4 range (`10.0.0.1-10.0.0.99`).

Address families are strict: an IPv4 pattern never matches an IPv6
address, so `0.0.0.0/0` does not quietly swallow IPv6.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> dstaddr.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> DstAddr.<op>
type: REG_SZ, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list meaning any of
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, Present; an address, a CIDR prefix, or an IPv4 range
applies: live — at the next generation

The destination IP address.

Values: an exact address (`10.0.0.5`, `fd00::1`), a CIDR prefix
(`10.0.0.0/8`, `fd00::/64`), or an IPv4 range (`10.0.0.1-10.0.0.99`).

Address families are strict: an IPv4 pattern never matches an IPv6
address, so `0.0.0.0/0` does not quietly swallow IPv6.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> protocol.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Protocol.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; a number, or a name
applies: live — at the next generation

The IP protocol.

Values: a number, or `tcp`, `udp`, `icmp`, `icmpv6`, `sctp`.

Present on any IP packet.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> ttl.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Ttl.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; a TTL or hop limit
applies: live — at the next generation

The packet's remaining time to live.

Present on any IP packet. Not a `Flow` fact -- it changes per packet, so
a flow has no one value for it.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> dscp.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Dscp.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; 0-63
applies: live — at the next generation

The packet's DSCP field.

Present on any IP packet. Not a `Flow` fact.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> fragment.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Fragment.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; 0 or 1
applies: live — at the next generation

Whether the packet is a fragment.

Only ever 1 before defragmentation, which means only at the `RawPacket`
seat. At the `Packet` layer everything is reassembled and this is always
0. Not a `Flow` fact.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> srcport.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> SrcPort.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; 0-65535
applies: live — at the next generation

The source port.

Present on TCP, UDP and SCTP.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> dstport.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> DstPort.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; 0-65535
applies: live — at the next generation

The destination port.

Present on TCP, UDP and SCTP.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> tcpflags.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> TcpFlags.<op>
type: REG_SZ, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list of flag names
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, Has, Hasnt, Present; a flag name, or a list
applies: live — at the next generation

The TCP header flags.

Values: `FIN`, `SYN`, `RST`, `PSH`, `ACK`, `URG`, `ECE`, `CWR`.

`Has` requires every listed bit set and `Hasnt` every listed bit clear;
that is usually what you want, rather than `Equal`, which pins the whole
field.

Present on TCP. Not a `Flow` fact.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> icmptype.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> IcmpType.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; an ICMP or ICMPv6 type
applies: live — at the next generation

The ICMP message type.

Present on ICMP and ICMPv6.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> icmpcode.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> IcmpCode.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; an ICMP or ICMPv6 code
applies: live — at the next generation

The ICMP message code.

Present on ICMP and ICMPv6.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> length.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Length.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; a length in bytes
applies: live — at the next generation

The packet's length at this seat, as the stack sees it.

Present at every layer, but not a `Flow` fact -- a flow has no single
length.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> flowstate.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> FlowState.<op>
type: REG_SZ, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list meaning any of
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, Present; new, established, related, invalid, untracked
applies: live — at the next generation

What conntrack makes of this packet.

Values: `new`, `established`, `related`, `invalid`, `untracked`.

The `Packet` layer only, at its proper seat. The `RawPacket` seat stands
before conntrack has run, and the `Flow` layer is the judgment of a
flow, so neither has it.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> related.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Related.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; 0 or 1
applies: live — at the next generation

Whether this flow was expected by another.

An ICMP error for a live flow, or an FTP data connection. The `Flow`
layer only.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> time.year.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Time.Year.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; a year
applies: live — at the next generation

The year of the wall clock, UTC.

Present at every layer. In a `Flow` rule, consulting a `Time.*` fact
expires the flow's sentence at that fact's next flip, so the flow is
re-judged rather than keeping a verdict the clock has outrun.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> time.month.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Time.Month.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; 1-12
applies: live — at the next generation

The month of the wall clock, UTC.

Present at every layer. In a `Flow` rule, consulting a `Time.*` fact
expires the flow's sentence at that fact's next flip, so the flow is
re-judged rather than keeping a verdict the clock has outrun.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> time.dayofmonth.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Time.DayOfMonth.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; 1-31
applies: live — at the next generation

The day of the month of the wall clock, UTC.

Present at every layer. In a `Flow` rule, consulting a `Time.*` fact
expires the flow's sentence at that fact's next flip, so the flow is
re-judged rather than keeping a verdict the clock has outrun.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> time.dayofweek.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Time.DayOfWeek.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; 1-7, ISO
applies: live — at the next generation

The day of the week of the wall clock, UTC.

Values: 1-7, ISO (1 = Monday, 7 = Sunday).

Present at every layer. In a `Flow` rule, consulting a `Time.*` fact
expires the flow's sentence at that fact's next flip, so the flow is
re-judged rather than keeping a verdict the clock has outrun.

