--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> network.id.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Network.Id.<op>
type: REG_SZ, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list meaning any of
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, Present; the record's key name under Networks\
applies: live — at the next generation

Which network the machine is standing on.

Present at every layer once a network has been identified on the
interface the packet crossed. netd writes the record; the kernel reads
it as the network context.

This is what lets one policy behave differently at home and in a cafe
without being rewritten.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> network.name.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Network.Name.<op>
type: REG_SZ, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list meaning any of
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, Present; the operator's Name on the record
applies: live — at the next generation

The operator's name for the network.

Present at every layer, when the network record carries a `Name`.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> network.trust.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Network.Trust.<op>
type: REG_SZ, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list meaning any of
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, Present; the operator's Trust on the record
applies: live — at the next generation

How far the operator said this network should be trusted.

Present at every layer, when the network record carries a `Trust`.

It is your word, not evidence: a rule conditioning on it is stating that
you accept the identification netd made.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> network.kind.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Network.Kind.<op>
type: REG_SZ, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list meaning any of
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this fact)
valid: Equal, Present; as Interface.Kind
applies: live — at the next generation

What kind of interface the network was seen on.

Values: as `Interface.Kind`: `wired`, `wireless`, `loopback`, ....

Present at every layer, under the same conditions as `Network.Id`.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> tag.<name>.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Tag.<name>.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on this tag)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; the tag's value
applies: live — at the next generation

Reads the flow tag `<name>`, which some rule's `TAG` effect wrote.

Tags are how one judgment tells a later one what it decided. The name is
yours: whatever a rule writes with `TAG`, another reads here. A tag no
rule anywhere writes is not an error, but a read of it can only ever be
absent.

Which tags a layer may read is the one place the layering shows.
`RawPacket` may write tags and read none. `Packet` may read tags written
by `RawPacket` or `Packet` rules. `Flow` may read any tag. Reading
downwards — a `Packet` rule reading a tag a `Flow` rule wrote — refuses
the generation.

A flow carries at most 64 distinct tags; past that the write is refused
and counted. There is no tag store at the `Interface` layer at all, so a
`Tag.*` condition there refuses the generation.

--- machine\system\network\rules\<layer>\<rule...> counter.<view>.<op>
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Rules\<Layer>\<rule...> Counter.<view>.<op>
type: REG_DWORD, REG_QWORD, a decimal string, or a REG_MULTI_SZ list
default: (absent — the rule does not condition on a counter)
valid: Equal, GreaterThan, LessThan, Present; a count
applies: live — at the next generation

Reads a counter view over the stream some rule's `COUNT` effect writes.

`<view>` is a stream name, optionally with arguments in parentheses: a
window (`10s`, `1h`, at most `1d`; omitted means the cumulative total)
and a key (`SrcAddr`, `DstAddr`, `Interface`, or a `+`-compound; omitted
means one global cell). So `Counter.dns` is the running total,
`Counter.dns(10s)` the last ten seconds, and `Counter.dns(10s,SrcAddr)`
the last ten seconds per source.

This is what a rate limit is made of: count in one rule, read the count
in another. A view over a stream no rule writes is statically dead and
refuses the generation; a stream nobody reads is fine.

Windows are approximated with eight buckets, so a value can be up to an
eighth of a window stale. A counter table holds 4096 keys; past that
idle keys are reaped, and if none are idle the new key is refused and
counted. As with tags there is no store at the `Interface` layer, so a
`Counter.*` condition there refuses the generation.

**A note on spelling.** The argument list is parsed with whitespace
trimmed, so `Counter.dns(1h,SrcAddr)` and `Counter.dns(1h, SrcAddr)` are
the same view. This page cannot be found by the spelling with a space in
it — a regman lookup never reads past one — so write arguments without
spaces if you want the value name you wrote to be one you can look up.
